# ⚙️ What Happens When systemd Hits a Failing Unit on a Containerized Node?

---

# Flow

```text id="pk16zm"
systemd unit fails
        ↓
Restart policy triggered
        ↓
systemd retries restart
        ↓
Failure threshold exceeded
        ↓
Unit enters failed state
```

---

# 🚑 Auto-Recovery Strategy

## 1. Configure Restart Policy

Example:

```ini id="ix4u8d"
Restart=always
RestartSec=5
```

---

## 2. Node Health Monitoring

Use:

* node-problem-detector
* Prometheus alerts

---

## 3. Auto Remediation

If kubelet/container runtime unhealthy:

* cordon node
* drain node
* ASG replaces node

---

## 4. Immutable Infrastructure

Never manually repair nodes.

Instead:

* replace failed node automatically
* bootstrap using Launch Templates/cloud-init

---

# 🔥 Detect & Mitigate Pod-to-Pod Lateral Movement

---

# Detection Strategy

## Runtime Security

Use:

* Falco
* Tetragon
* Cilium Hubble

Detect:

* suspicious exec
* shell spawning
* privilege escalation
* unexpected network traffic

---

# Mitigation Strategy

## 1. Network Policies

Default deny:

```text id="1xllwy"
deny-all
```

Then explicitly allow required traffic.

---

## 2. Service Mesh

Use:

* Istio
* Linkerd

for:

* mTLS
* traffic policies
* identity enforcement

---

## 3. Namespace Segmentation

Separate:

* apps
* infra
* monitoring
* prod/dev

---

## 4. Pod Security Standards

Prevent:

* privileged containers
* host networking
* host PID access

---

## 5. EDR/SIEM Integration

Integrate:

* GuardDuty
* Sentinel
* Splunk
* Elastic SIEM

